"""Reader reference material and assessment, distinct from planned chapters."""
EXERCISES = [
('conceptual','C1','A program reports “saved” after adding a task to a map. Which contract can it satisfy, and which contract does it fail?',
 'It can satisfy a session-only create-and-list contract under sequential execution. It fails the chosen process-restart persistence promise because the next process constructs a new map. Renaming the map a repository or putting it behind an interface changes organization, not persistence.'),
('conceptual','C2','How does a method signature differ from a behavioral contract?',
 'The signature identifies inputs and outputs at the type level. The contract also states validation, side effects, errors, commit timing, retry behavior, and concurrency or lifetime assumptions. Two insert(title) methods can be incompatible if one returns before commit and the other promises persistence.'),
('conceptual','C3','Does low coupling require removing all dependencies?',
 'No. Useful behavior requires collaboration. Reduce unnecessary knowledge and make necessary obligations explicit. One dependency on a global mutable schema can cause more change propagation than several narrow stable interfaces. An interface that hides neither knowledge nor responsibility may merely add indirection.'),
('conceptual','C4','Are HLD, LLD, and deployment separate formal categories of software?',
 'They are common organizing viewpoints with community-dependent boundaries. HLD and LLD describe consequential structure at different detail; deployment locates runtime artifacts. They must agree about responsibility, ordering, and placement. A class diagram does not establish host redundancy.'),
('conceptual','C5','How can a system be available but lose data, or durable but temporarily unavailable?',
 'An application can keep accepting requests while acknowledging only volatile state, then lose that state after restart. A store can retain every committed row while refusing requests during recovery. Uptime measurements alone do not demonstrate durability, and retained data alone does not establish serving availability.'),
('scenario','S1','The database commits task 7; the success response is lost; the client retries. Can the client safely assume the first operation failed?',
 'No. The timeout provides insufficient evidence about commit. A second unkeyed create may produce task 8. To bind repeated attempts to one intent, define a stable operation key, request fingerprint, atomic result storage, and retention behavior. Blind retries and a label saying “exactly once” do not supply that contract.'),
('scenario','S2','Two adapters implement the same 80-character title limit. It changes to 200, but only one adapter is edited. What design change addresses the cause?',
 'Put the authoritative rule in the shared task service and have both adapters call it. Adapters still validate transport syntax and bound payloads. Repeating the edit in both adapters repairs this instance but preserves the duplication risk. Moving both adapters into different services would make coordinated changes harder.'),
('scenario','S3','The team adds a second application host, each with its own local task database. What new failure has been introduced?',
 'Requests routed to different hosts can observe different task sets. Local persistence is not shared state or replication. The prior single-host assumption has changed, so review data placement, consistency, identity generation, and failure behavior. A load balancer cannot merge the two databases.'),
('design','D1','Review this design: HTTP handler → shared dictionary → async file writer; the handler returns success immediately. Draw the failure and propose the smallest change that meets the restart promise.',
 'Failure trace: mutate dictionary, return success, crash before async writer commits; restart loses an acknowledged task. The minimal correction is to wait for an appropriate durable commit before reporting success, propagating write failures. A transaction-aware local repository is one feasible implementation. A queue alone only moves the durability boundary; its acknowledgment semantics would still need justification. Keep the modules in one deployment unless another requirement calls for distribution.'),
('implementation','I1','Run examples/todo.py. Add a failing repository and prove that a failed insert never returns a Task. Then reopen the real store and prove a successful task remains.',
 'The service must propagate the repository exception; it must not manufacture a success object after failure. Use a repository whose insert raises OSError and assert create raises. With SQLiteTasks, create a task, close the connection, open a new connection to the same temporary path, and compare the stored record. Invalid titles must not insert any row. These tests exercise different promises; an in-memory double cannot demonstrate real persistence.'),
('thought_experiment','T1','The business now requires “no acknowledged task lost after the only disk is destroyed.” Can the current ADR stand? What evidence would a replacement need?',
 'The current ADR explicitly assumes intact committed storage, so its design is insufficient for the new requirement. Consider independent durable copies, acknowledgment rules, geographic or device failure correlation, backups versus live replication, and tested recovery. Asynchronous copying can lose recently acknowledged writes; copying corruption can preserve the wrong state. A replacement must specify the failure model and acceptable recovery behavior, then test its claims. More replicas without acknowledgment and independence rules do not establish the guarantee.')
]

# term, meaning, target; entries are definitions, not claims that later chapters exist.
GLOSSARY = [
('Abstraction','A behavioral view that hides details a collaborator does not need, while retaining relevant obligations.','ch-1-2'),
('ADR','Architecture Decision Record: one consequential choice, its context, rationale, status, and consequences.','ch-1-3'),
('Application architecture','The organization and interaction of application responsibilities and their dependency rules.','unit-7'),
('Availability','Ability to provide the specified useful response to eligible requests under stated conditions.','ch-1-3'),
('Backpressure','A mechanism that communicates or enforces downstream capacity limits on upstream work.','unit-4'),
('Boundary','A chosen division of responsibility, trust, execution, or representation. State which kind.','ch-1-1'),
('CAP','Under a network partition, the theorem rules out simultaneously guaranteeing its formal consistency and availability properties. It is not a general “pick any two” menu.','unit-14'),
('Cohesion','How closely a module’s elements contribute to a focused responsibility.','ch-1-2'),
('Component','A unit of responsibility in a chosen design view; not necessarily a process or service.','ch-1-1'),
('Composition root','The part of the program that constructs and connects concrete collaborators.','ch-1-2'),
('Concurrency','Multiple activities in progress with potentially interleaved execution; parallel execution is not required.','unit-4'),
('Consistency model','A contract specifying which observations of shared or replicated state are allowed.','unit-14'),
('Container (C4)','An application or data store in C4 terminology; not necessarily an OS container.','ch-1-4'),
('Contract','An interface’s behavioral obligations, including inputs, outcomes, errors, state effects, and timing assumptions.','ch-1-1'),
('Coupling','What an element depends on or must know about another element.','ch-1-2'),
('Dependency injection','Supplying collaborators to an object or function rather than having it locate or construct them internally.','unit-6'),
('Durability','Persistence of committed state under an explicitly stated set of failure assumptions.','ch-1-3'),
('Encapsulation','Controlling access to a representation so behavior and invariants cannot be casually bypassed.','ch-1-2'),
('Error budget','The allowed unreliability implied by a chosen SLO over its measurement window.','unit-2'),
('Eventual consistency','A convergence property under the model’s assumptions; it alone gives neither an immediate freshness bound nor a complete conflict policy.','unit-14'),
('Exactly-once processing','A scoped guarantee about effects or records within a defined system boundary and assumptions. It does not automatically include arbitrary external side effects.','unit-16'),
('Failure domain','Elements exposed to a common cause of failure, such as a process, host, disk, or region.','unit-17'),
('HLD','High-Level Design: a common view of major components, state placement, communication, and system qualities.','ch-1-1'),
('Idempotency','Repeated application of an operation has the same relevant effect as one application, under its defined identity and scope.','unit-8'),
('Interface','The operations and data forms exposed to a collaborator; behavior needs an accompanying contract.','ch-1-1'),
('Invariant','A condition required to hold in every valid system state.','ch-1-1'),
('Latency','Elapsed time for an operation under a specified measurement boundary.','unit-2'),
('Linearizability','A correctness condition in which operations appear atomic and respect real-time precedence.','unit-14'),
('LLD','Low-Level Design: a common view of internal modules, types, interfaces, algorithms, and collaboration.','ch-1-1'),
('Modularity','Separating responsibilities with explicit interfaces and controlled dependencies.','ch-1-2'),
('MVCC','Multiversion concurrency control: retaining versions to help coordinate concurrent database access. Exact visibility and isolation depend on implementation.','unit-10'),
('Nonfunctional requirement','A constraint on the quality or conditions of system behavior, such as latency or persistence.','ch-1-3'),
('Quorum','A set of participants whose responses meet a protocol’s decision rule. Intersection alone does not establish all read or write correctness properties.','unit-15'),
('Repository','A component that mediates access to a data model through an explicit contract. Its transaction ownership must be specified.','ch-1-2'),
('RPO','Recovery Point Objective: an objective for the amount of data loss measured in time.','unit-17'),
('RTO','Recovery Time Objective: an objective for restoring service after disruption.','unit-17'),
('Saga','A coordinated sequence of local transactions with compensating actions; compensation need not be a perfect inverse.','unit-16'),
('Serializability','A transactional correctness condition requiring equivalence to some serial execution; real-time order is a separate issue.','unit-10'),
('SLI / SLO / SLA','Indicator: measured behavior. Objective: target for that behavior. Agreement: an explicit service agreement, potentially with consequences.','unit-2'),
('State','Information whose current value influences subsequent system behavior.','ch-1-1'),
('Throughput','Completed work per unit time under a specified workload and success definition.','unit-2'),
('Trade-off','A choice among competing consequences evaluated against requirements and assumptions.','ch-1-3'),
('Transactional outbox','Recording a state change and publication intent in one local transaction, then relaying the intent; duplicate delivery still requires handling.','unit-16'),
('Trust boundary','A point across which identity, privilege, or confidence in input changes.','unit-18'),
]

FORMULAS = [
('State transition','T(S, c) → (S′, result)','Identify valid state changes and preserved invariants. This abstracts away implementation and failures.','ch-1-1','introduced'),
('Weighted preference','score(a) = Σ wᵢ sᵢ(a) / Σ wᵢ','Use nonnegative weights with positive total. Compare only feasible designs. Scores are illustrative judgments, not benchmark results.','ch-1-3','introduced'),
('Request rate','λ = daily users × requests per user per day / 86,400','An average workload estimate. Peak factors must be assumed or measured separately.','unit-2','planned'),
('Little’s Law','L = λ W','Long-run stable averages with consistent boundaries and units. It is not a universal p99 prediction.','unit-2','planned'),
('Amdahl’s Law','speedup(N) = 1 / [(1 − p) + p/N]','Idealized fixed workload and parallel fraction p; excludes extra coordination overhead unless modeled.','unit-2','planned'),
('Storage growth','bytes/day = records/day × bytes/record × retained copies','Account separately for indexes, compression, retention, metadata, and headroom.','unit-2','planned'),
('Error allowance','allowed bad events = (1 − target fraction) × eligible events','Valid for an event-based SLO; time-based objectives need their own denominator.','unit-19','planned'),
('Token refill','tokens′ = min(capacity, tokens + refill_rate × elapsed)','Admission consumes tokens atomically. Distributed clocks and state ownership need additional design.','ch-22-2','planned'),
('Quorum intersection','R + W > N','An intersection condition for reads and writes on a fixed replica set. Not by itself a proof of linearizability.','unit-15','planned'),
('Cost per outcome','cost / successful business outcomes','Define accounting period and include capacity, storage, networking, telemetry, and operational work where relevant.','unit-21','planned')
]

